    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">624</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> are defeated at Badr and several of their leaders are killed, among them Amr ibn Hisham</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">625</span><span class="rf-text">Abu Sufyan mobilises a force of over 3,000 men to retaliate for the losses at Badr</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 625</span><span class="rf-text">Muhammad yields to those who want to fight in the open, and the Muslims take position on the slopes of Uhud; Abd Allah ibn Ubayy withdraws with 300 men</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 625</span><span class="rf-text">Duels between standard-bearers open the fighting and the Meccan lines are pushed back</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 625</span><span class="rf-text">Most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> leave the hill, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>'s cavalry attacks the Muslim flank and rear; Hamza is killed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 625</span><span class="rf-text">Umar leads a counter-charge that dislodges <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid</a>'s forces from the slopes, and the fighting ceases</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">625</span><span class="rf-text">The Meccans halt at Hamra al-Asad, and Muhammad sends out a small force to scout them</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">627</span><span class="rf-text">Abu Sufyan and allied tribes attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trench">Battle of the Trench</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-uhud" data-v="e689b22e1f" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two armies face each other</b> The Muslims stood on the lower slopes of Uhud with the mountain behind them and 50 archers on a rocky hill to the west. The Meccans formed facing them, with the main body in the middle, a wing on each side and their camp behind.</li><li><b>Abu Amir leads the first Meccan charge</b> The Meccans opened with an initial charge led by Abu Amir, a Medinan exile, which advanced across the ground in front of the Muslim line.</li><li><b>The charge is thrown back with stones</b> The Muslims met Abu Amir and his men with a shower of stones. They were forced to retreat to the camps behind the Meccan lines.</li><li><b>The Meccans are pushed back</b> The Muslims advanced and drove the Meccan army back. Meccan cavalry tried several times to overrun the Muslim left flank, and the archers on the hill turned them away.</li><li><b>The archers leave the hill</b> The Muslims pierced the Meccan lines and victory seemed certain. Most of the archers disobeyed Muhammad's order to stay and ran down to join the advance, leaving the flank open.</li><li><b>Khalid's cavalry attacks the archers on the hill</b> The archers who had gone down made for the Meccan camp. Khalid ibn al-Walid's cavalry exploited the open flank and attacked the minority of archers who had refused to leave the hill.</li><li><b>The Muslim flank and rear are overrun</b> From the hill the Meccans were able to overrun the Muslim flank and rear. Confusion followed and numerous Muslims were killed.</li><li><b>The Muslims are broken and regroup higher up</b> Confusion followed and many Muslims were killed, Hamza among them. After hand-to-hand fighting many fled and regrouped higher up the slopes of Uhud, with the Meccan cavalry coming after them.</li><li><b>Umar's counter-charge clears the slopes</b> Umar ibn al-Khattab rallied troops at the base of the mountain and led a counter-charge on the Prophet's instruction. It drove Khalid's forces off the slopes, and the Meccan cavalry could not climb them after the Muslims, so the fighting ceased.</li><li><b>A small group is cut off on the way to Medina</b> A small group of Muslims was cut off and tried to make its way back to Medina, though many of them were killed.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the Muslim emigration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, hostilities with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> grew, largely because of Muslim raids on Meccan trade caravans. Francis Peters proposes that the raids were a quick remedy for the poverty of Muhammad's people in their new land, who lacked agricultural skills and capital for trade. Tariq <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a> agrees, and adds that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-badr">Battle of Badr</a> in particular was meant to impress the Meccans, who were growing more hostile towards Medina.</p><p>Muhammad learned of a large Quraysh caravan, 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> laden with 50,000 dinars, travelling from Gaza to Mecca, and ordered his followers to ambush it. Abu Sufyan, who led the caravan, got wind of the plan and sent for help from Mecca while the caravan took another route. At Badr the Muslims defeated the larger Meccan force. A number of influential and experienced Quraysh men were killed, including Amr ibn Hisham, and their prestige was shaken. Old tribal enemies such as the Hawazin began to look to them again. Muhammad used the victory as proof of his prophethood, and those who joined his raids grew more numerous.</p><p>Abu Sufyan, chosen as successor to the leader of the Quraysh, vowed vengeance. Some months after Badr he went to Medina with 200 men, burned two houses and laid waste to some fields to fulfil his vow, and left. A few months later he mobilised an invasion force of over 3,000 men.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Uhud&#8221; cites where it covers Background:</p><ul><li>F. Buhl, Muḥammad (Brill, 1993)</li><li>William Montgomery Watt, Muhammad: Prophet and Statesman (Oxford University Press, 1974)</li><li>Bernard Lewis, The Arabs in History (Oxford University Press, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Abu Sufyan ibn Harb led about 3,000 men towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, 700 of them equipped with chain mail. According to the early Muslim historian Ibn Ishaq, several Meccan women accompanied the army to raise morale, among them Abu Sufyan's wife Hind bint Utba. The Meccans camped on the pastures north of the city, hoping to draw the Muslims into open battle. On the field Abu Sufyan held the centre, Ikrima, son of Amr ibn Hisham, the left flank and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a> the right, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> commanded the cavalry and coordinated the attack of its two wings.</p><p>Muhammad had originally meant to stay in Medina and fight from its fortifications, aware that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> would struggle with a siege. Many Muslims who had missed Badr begged for an open battle, and he gave way and put on his armour. Three Muslim contingents totalling 1,000 men were assembled. Muhammad screened them in person and sent away those he judged too young, among them Abd Allah, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-al-khattab">Umar ibn al-Khattab</a>. Only 100 men had chain mail. Shortly before the fighting Abd Allah ibn Ubayy, chief of the Khazraj, withdrew with 300 men and returned to Medina, reportedly because he disliked the plan to march out; the Qur'an later censures him and his followers for it.</p><p>The remaining force, around 700, took position on the lower slopes of Uhud with the mountain at its back. Muhammad placed 50 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> on a rocky hill on the west side of the camp, to keep the Meccan cavalry, which outnumbered the Muslim horse fifty to one, from circling round the army.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Muhammad told the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> to keep their place whether the battle went for or against the army, so that the Muslims could not be attacked from their direction. The Meccan charge, led by the Medinan exile Abu Amir, was driven back by a shower of stones. The Meccan standard-bearer Talha ibn Abi Talha al-Abdari then challenged the enemy to a duel, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a> struck him down. Talha's brother Uthman took up the fallen banner and Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib killed him. Talha's brothers and sons came forward one by one until they had all perished. A general engagement followed, and the Muslims swept through the Meccan ranks.</p><p>The Meccan cavalry's repeated attempts on the left Muslim flank were beaten off by the archers, and the Muslims pierced the Meccan lines with victory seeming certain. Most of the archers then ran downhill to join the advance and plunder the Meccan camp, which disobeyed Muhammad's orders to stay put. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>'s cavalry attacked the few archers who held their place and then overran the Muslim flank and rear. In the confusion many Muslims were killed, Hamza among them, struck down by a javelin thrown by Wahshi ibn Harb. A rumour spread that Muhammad had been killed; he had been wounded in the face by stones.</p><p>Many Muslims fled and regrouped higher up the slopes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-al-khattab">Umar ibn al-Khattab</a> rallied them at the foot of the mountain and, on Muhammad's instruction to keep the Meccans from the high ground, led a counter-charge that dislodged Khalid's men. The cavalry could not climb the slopes, and the fighting ceased.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Uhud&#8221; cites where it covers Meccan march to Medina, Muslim encampment at Uhud, Duels, Meccan retreat and counter-attack:</p><ul><li>William Montgomery Watt, Muhammad: Prophet and Statesman (Oxford University Press, 1974)</li><li>Russ Rodgers, The Generalship of Muhammad: Battles and Campaigns of the Prophet of Allah (University Press of Florida, 2012)</li><li>Muhammad Ibn Ishaq, The Life of Muhammad: A Translation of Ishaq's Sirat Rasul Allah (Oxford University Press, 1955)</li><li>P. M. Holt (ed.), The Cambridge History of Islam: Volume 1A, The Central Islamic Lands from Pre-Islamic Times to the First World War (Cambridge University Press, 1977)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Before leaving, Abu Sufyan went up the mountain and called out that victory in war goes by turns and that the day was in exchange for Badr. By Ibn Ishaq's account Umar answered at Muhammad's bidding that the two sides were not equal, since their dead were in paradise and the Meccans' in hell. Abu Sufyan asked whether Muhammad was dead, and Umar replied that he could hear every word. Abu Sufyan then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> without attacking the wounded Muslims again.</p><p>Ibn al-Athir names 85 Muslims killed, 75 of them Medinans (43 from the Banu Khazraj and 32 from the Banu Aws) and 10 Emigrants from Mecca; 46 of the 85 had fought at Badr. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> in Sahih al-Bukhari puts the dead at seventy. Hind bint Utba and her companions are said to have mutilated the Muslim dead, and Hind is reported to have cut open Hamza's body and tried to eat his liver. The Muslims buried their dead on the field and went home that evening.</p><p>The Meccans halted that night at Hamra al-Asad, a few miles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. Next morning Muhammad sent a small force to scout them; according to Watt he judged that a show of force was needed to speed them away. The Meccans, unwilling to look chased off, stayed nearby for a few days. Al-Waqidi has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> say that the Meccans left because they had many wounded and all their horses had been wounded by arrows, and because they feared that Ibn Ubayy and others who had held back might attack them.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Uhud&#8221; cites where it covers Meccan retreat and counter-attack, Aftermath, Further conflict, Muslim casualties:</p><ul><li>William Montgomery Watt, Muhammad: Prophet and Statesman (Oxford University Press, 1974)</li><li>P. M. Holt (ed.), The Cambridge History of Islam: Volume 1A, The Central Islamic Lands from Pre-Islamic Times to the First World War (Cambridge University Press, 1977)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Most of what is known comes from the sira and maghazi traditions, the biographical narratives and campaign accounts of the early Muslim centuries. Ibn Ishaq's biography of Muhammad gives the main narrative, and the general sequence of events gained consensus early. Accounts of the battle derive mainly from descendants of the participants. Al-Waqidi and al-Ya'qubi also give accounts, and they differ on who stayed beside Muhammad in the crisis: al-Ya'qubi names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, Zubayr ibn al-Awwam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talha-ibn-ubayd-allah">Talha ibn Ubayd Allah</a>, al-Waqidi fourteen companions, seven Emigrants and seven Ansar, and he records another account in which eight companions swore to fight to the death.</p><p>The Qur'an refers to the battle in verses such as 3:122, 3:152 and 3:166 to 168, and Safi al-Rahman al-Mubarakpuri connects 8:36 with it; Ibn Kathir's commentary on that verse, citing Ibn Ishaq, ties it to the wealth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> men spent to fight Muhammad. Sahih al-Bukhari preserves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> under Abd Allah ibn Jubayr (3039) and on those who turned back (1884). All of these are Islamic sources, and the Meccan side is known only through them.</p><p>Chase F. Robinson judges the basic narrative and chronology reasonably authentic, but finds some of the more elaborate details, such as the exact scale of the Muslim defeat, doubtful or hard to establish. Historians also disagree on the outcome: Robinson holds that a disheartening Muslim defeat is clear enough, while Watt notes that the Meccans failed in their strategic aim and that their withdrawal suggests weakness.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is seen as a significant setback for the Muslims and a minor victory for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, who returned with an even larger force in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trench">Battle of the Trench</a> in 627. Abu Sufyan, whose leadership was no longer disputed, began building alliances with nomadic tribes. Two Muslim parties were killed in ambushes, among them instructors led into a trap by guides of a tribe that had asked for them. Muhammad then became convinced that the Banu Nadir were plotting against him, and after a fifteen-day siege they were expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>.</p><p>For the Muslims the battle had a religious as well as a military side. They had expected another victory like Badr, which they took as a sign of God's favour. A verse of the Qur'an revealed soon after, 3:152, names disobedience and desire for loot as the cause of the setback, and the tradition reads the losses as partly a punishment and partly a test of steadfastness. Firestone observes that such verses gave the Muslims inspiration and hope and made their later battles sacred, and that the battle seemed to strengthen their solidarity rather than demoralise them. It is also noted for the emergence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>, who later became one of the best known Arab generals in the conquests of the Sassanid Empire and of Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><p>A modern military assessment praises Muhammad's choice of ground. The front was about 730 to 820 metres wide, with Mount Einein on one flank and the defiles of Uhud on the other, so that only the approach guarded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> lay open to the Meccan cavalry. The cave on Mount Uhud where Muhammad rested has drawn attention since some Salafi scholars proposed destroying it; local authorities rejected the calls and moved to protect the site.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Uhud in March 625 the Meccans under Abu Sufyan fought the Muslims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> under Muhammad. The Muslims were winning until most of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> left the hill and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>'s cavalry struck the rear. Scholars differ on whether it was a defeat or an indecisive result.</em></p>

